// swift-rt.metajs -- SWIFT'S OWN SEMANTICS, in MetaJS. Layer 2 of
// docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3, for the third language after the Lua pilot
// and PHP.
//
// languages/swift-to-llvm-ir.abnf emits handle IR that calls 66 js_* externs for
// its own full-syntax ratchet (66 over every swift test in tests/ as well - the
// union adds nothing). 35 of them are generic primitives languages/lib/runtime.c
// already provides (the C floor, layer 1). The other 31 are here.
//
// Compile with:
//     mec languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf languages/lib/swift-rt.metajs -q -rt-lib
// which is what tests/gen-swift-rt-ll.sh does; the result is the checked-in
// languages/lib/swift-rt.ll that swift-to-llvm-ir.abnf links next to runtime.ll.
// -rt-lib exports every TOP-LEVEL `function js_*` below under its own C symbol,
// so nothing else in this file is visible to the linker - the helpers are
// deliberately named sw*.
//
// THE AUTHORITATIVE SPECIFICATION IS abnf/jsrtswift.go plus the shared externs it
// layers over in abnf/jsrt.go (memberCall, js_pyget/js_pyset/js_pylen,
// js_is_type, js_jadd, js_char/js_char_code/js_kindex, js_dict_new), and this
// file matches them function for function. Where it CANNOT, the site says so in
// full.
//
// ----- the value model -------------------------------------------------------
//
//     null / undefined     ==  Swift nil. There is no Optional BOX in this
//                              model, deliberately and in both halves: Int? is
//                              just the value or nil.
//     a boolean            ==  Bool
//     a PLAIN NUMBER       ==  an Int whose value is exact in a double
//     a SIZED INTEGER      ==  an Int outside [-2^53, 2^53], or any value of a
//                              declared width (Int8 ... UInt64). The Go twin uses
//                              jsGInt (abnf/jsrtint.go); the C floor has the same
//                              thing as a REAL PRIMITIVE TYPE (tag 13) reached
//                              through the sint/sintOp/sintCmp/... host builtins.
//     a BOXED DOUBLE       ==  Double (and Float, which this subset aliases to
//                              Double at 64 bit precision - see jsrtswift.go).
//                              The Go twin uses jsJFlo (abnf/jsrtjvm.go); the C
//                              floor has it as tag 14, reached through
//                              flo/floIs/floNum/... Swift builds it with STYLE 0
//                              and never renders through floStr: every Swift
//                              print path goes through swRender, whose float arm
//                              is swFloStr, Swift's own SwiftDtoa rules.
//     a string             ==  String
//     an array             ==  Array
//     a {__dict} object    ==  Dictionary: keys/vals parallel arrays, INSERTION
//                              ordered (Swift's own order is the hash order and
//                              is unspecified, so any fixed order is as correct).
//     a {__tuple: n} obj   ==  a tuple; the slots are "0".."n-1" and the labels,
//                              when it has any, ride along as __tlabels.
//     a {__ecase} object   ==  an enum case: __ename, __vals, __labels.
//     a {__set} object     ==  a Set literal.
//     a {__isclass} /
//       {__isenum} object  ==  a TYPE DESCRIPTOR: __name, __super, __struct,
//                              __fields.
//     any other object     ==  an instance; its descriptor is __class.
//
// ----- BOTH tags were already in the floor, and that is the whole story -------
//
// PHP was picked second because it is the one language whose Go twin uses zero
// jsJFlo. Swift is the FIRST language to need both floor primitives at once -
// tag 13 for Int8...UInt64 and tag 14 for Double - and it needed no new code for
// either: sint*/flo* ARE Swift's two numeric types. There is no i64 pair layer in
// this file, where lua-rt.metajs needed ~200 lines of one, and no {__flo} object
// box, where php-rt.metajs needed a {__pf} one. That is the sized-integer and
// boxed-double tags paying for themselves the second and first time respectively.
//
// ----- what Swift did NOT need, which is a finding of its own -----------------
//
//  1. NO `__raw` truthy shim. compile-core's truthy() emits `icmp ne <call>, 0`
//     on core.truthyExt, so that extern must answer a RAW INTEGER - and a shim
//     handing back a boolean HANDLE gives 2 for false, which is != 0, so every
//     condition in the program would be taken, silently. Swift is the first of
//     the remaining languages that is IMMUNE: it leaves core.truthyExt at its
//     default "js_truthy", which is the C FLOOR'S OWN function and already
//     answers the raw 1/0. Nothing here needs the suffix. Checked, not assumed:
//     `js_truthy` is in the module's declare list and in runtime.c.
//  2. NO `_raw` PARAMETERS. Every operator selector the swift emitter passes -
//     js_swarith's and js_swshift's op, js_swintconv's width and signedness - is
//     emitted with emitStr/emitNum or as a real boolean handle, never with a bare
//     handle(k). Like PHP and unlike Lua, Swift has zero of them.
//  3. NO jsChar. js_char / js_char_code / js_kindex are Kotlin's Char externs and
//     the floor has no jsChar, which is why js_jadd and js_jfstep were left out
//     of it. Swift reaches all three, but ONLY inside the emitter's own
//     grapheme-cluster walk (sw_gcount / sw_nfd), where the value produced by
//     js_char is immediately handed to js_jadd for CONCATENATION and the value
//     produced by js_kindex is immediately handed to js_char_code. In Go that
//     round trip is jsChar -> rt.toString -> the character, and jsChar ->
//     rt.toNumber -> the code point. A one-character STRING makes exactly the
//     same two round trips, so Swift needs no new floor tag. Written out because
//     the next language may reach js_char somewhere this equivalence does not
//     hold, and then it is a real floor gap.
//
// ----- WALL 3, the scope chain: lowered in the EMITTER, not here --------------
//
// js_kget and js_kset used to be externs that took a SCOPE HANDLE and walked the
// chain with a `this` fallback. A scope is the floor's private storage - which is
// why the Lua pilot put js_scope_decl/set/typeof INTO the floor - so layer 2
// cannot open one, and neither could ever have been written here. Both are pure
// PROBES, and an EMITTER may call js_scope_typeof / js_scope_get / js_scope_set,
// so swift-to-llvm-ir.abnf now emits them as the IR helpers sw_kget / sw_kset.
// The extern count went 68 -> 66 and llvm.Run stayed at 209/209. This is the
// generalisation PHP recorded, and Swift is its second confirmation.
//
// ----- MetaJS rules that shape the code below --------------------------------
//
// docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md: a local (or a reassigned parameter) keeps the
// TYPE CLASS it is first assigned, so a variable that holds a number in one
// branch and an object in another is declared `var x = anytype`; there is no ASI,
// so every `return` carries its value on the same line and a value-less early
// return is BRACED; no for..in, no splice, no split/join/lastIndexOf, indexOf
// ignores its from-index, array.length is not assignable, and there are no
// exponent literals.

// The shared layer-2 runtime (docs/runtime-merge-plan.md part A). It supplies
// the externs every language implements identically, parameterised by the rtk*
// hooks below. Same mechanism ruby uses for `import "./regex.js"`.
import "./runtime.metajs"

// ----- the rtk* hooks the shared runtime calls -------------------------------
function rtkStr(v) { return swJavaStr(v) }
function rtkNum(v) { return swNum(v) }
function rtkIsChar(v) { return false }
function rtkIsArr(v) { return swIsArr(v) }

// ===================================================== the two number tags ==
//
// swWhole is jsrtswift.go's swIsWhole: a sized-integer box, or a Double whose
// value is a whole number. `typeof v == "number"` is TRUE for BOTH tags, so
// every site that meant "an ordinary double" has to say so - which is what the
// floIs test below is for.
function swWhole(v) {
    if (sintIs(v)) { return true }
    if (floIs(v)) { return false }
    if (typeof v != "number") { return false }
    if (v !== v) { return false }
    if (v === Infinity) { return false }
    if (0 - v === Infinity) { return false }
    return v === Math.trunc(v)
}

// swIsNumeric: does this value take part in numeric equality at all, so that
// 1.0 == "x" is false rather than 1.0 == 0.
function swIsNumeric(v) {
    return typeof v == "number"
}

// giIsIntegral: the narrower test the ordered comparisons need - a float operand
// must NOT be truncated into an integer comparison.
function swIntegral(v) {
    if (sintIs(v)) { return true }
    if (floIs(v)) { return false }
    return typeof v == "number"
}

// swNum is giFloat, the plain-number reading of any operand: sintNum is the
// UNSIGNED-aware one an UInt64 box needs (int64(-1) read as a uint64 is 1.8e19),
// and it falls through to to_number for everything else.
function swNum(v) { return sintNum(v) }

// swMkFlo is jsJFlo{f: x}: style 0, which is never consulted - see the header.
function swMkFlo(x) { return flo(x, 0) }

// Double.rounded(), i.e. .toNearestOrAwayFromZero, which is Go's math.Round and
// NOT JS's Math.round: (-2.5).rounded() is -3 and Math.round(-2.5) is -2.
// Three guards, all of them measured rather than assumed (see the probe in
// docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3):
//   - a ZERO returns itself, so (-0.0).rounded() keeps its sign. `x < 0` is FALSE
//     for -0.0, so without this the value would come back through
//     Math.floor(0.5) as +0.0 and print "0.0" where swift prints "-0.0". This was
//     the ONE line of a 1,910 line probe on which the native binary and llvm.Run
//     disagreed.
//   - |x| >= 2^52: every double that large is already an integer, and x + 0.5
//     would round UP to the next one.
//   - NaN and the infinities return themselves.
function swRound(x) {
    if (x !== x) { return x }
    if (x === 0) { return x }
    if (x === Infinity) { return x }
    if (-x === Infinity) { return x }
    var a = x
    if (a < 0) { a = -a }
    if (a >= 4503599627370496) { return x }
    if (x < 0) { return -Math.floor(a + 0.5) }
    return Math.floor(a + 0.5)
}

// ===================================================== float rendering ======
//
// swFloStr is Swift's Double.description (SwiftDtoa): the shortest decimal that
// round-trips, written as a plain decimal when the scientific exponent is in
// -4 ... 15 and in computerized scientific notation - two digit minimum exponent,
// always signed - outside it, with the integral case always carrying a ".0". So
// 1e15 prints 1000000000000000.0 and 1e16 prints 1e+16. The infinities are
// "inf"/"-inf" and the NaN is "nan". This is the twin of swFloStr in
// abnf/jsrtswift.go and of the same name in swift-interpreter.abnf.
function swFloStr(d) {
    if (d !== d) { return "nan" }
    if (d === Infinity) { return "inf" }
    if (0 - d === Infinity) { return "-inf" }
    var neg = swSignBit(d)
    var a = d
    // UNARY minus, not `0 - d`: the floor's d_neg flips the SIGN BIT, where
    // 0.0 - 0.0 is +0.0. This is the "a negative floating point literal does not
    // survive" trap of docs/runtime-next-plan.md, met from the other side.
    if (neg) { a = -d }
    var s = swFloDigits(a)
    if (neg) { return "-" + s }
    return s
}

// The sign of a zero, which a comparison cannot see: -0.0 prints "-0.0" in Swift.
// 1/x is -Infinity for -0 and +Infinity for +0.
function swSignBit(d) {
    if (d < 0) { return true }
    if (d === 0) { return 1 / d < 0 }
    return false
}

// swFloDigits renders a >= 0. The Go twin formats from strconv's 'e' form, which
// hands it the shortest round-tripping digit run and the scientific exponent
// directly. MetaJS has no such formatter, so the same pair is recovered from the
// engine's own shortest-round-trip rendering ("" + a) - which is jsNumString in
// the Go half and d_num_str in the floor, and both are the SAME shortest form
// strconv produces. swSplit10 below is the recovery.
function swFloDigits(a) {
    if (a === 0) { return "0.0" }
    var p = swSplit10(a)
    var digits = p.d
    var e10 = p.e
    if (e10 < 0 - 4 || e10 >= 16) {
        var out = digits.substring(0, 1)
        if (digits.length > 1) { out = out + "." + digits.substring(1, digits.length) }
        var sign = "+"
        var ex = e10
        if (ex < 0) {
            sign = "-"
            ex = 0 - ex
        }
        if (ex < 10) { return out + "e" + sign + "0" + ex }
        return out + "e" + sign + ex
    }
    var pt = e10 + 1                       // digits before the decimal point
    if (pt <= 0) { return "0." + swZeros(0 - pt) + digits }
    if (pt >= digits.length) { return digits + swZeros(pt - digits.length) + ".0" }
    return digits.substring(0, pt) + "." + digits.substring(pt, digits.length)
}

function swZeros(n) {
    var out = ""
    var i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        out = out + "0"
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// swSplit10 turns a > 0 into {d: the significant digits with no point and no
// leading or trailing zero, e: the SCIENTIFIC exponent} - i.e. the value is
// 0.d1d2... * 10^(e+1), the same pair strconv.FormatFloat(a, 'e', -1, 64) hands
// the Go twin. The input is the engine's own shortest rendering, which is either
// a plain decimal ("1234.5", "0.00012") or an exponent form ("1e+21", "1.5e-7").
function swSplit10(a) {
    var s = "" + a
    var mant = s
    var e10 = 0
    var ei = s.indexOf("e")
    if (ei >= 0) {
        mant = s.substring(0, ei)
        var es = s.substring(ei + 1, s.length)
        var eneg = false
        if (es.charAt(0) == "+") { es = es.substring(1, es.length) }
        else if (es.charAt(0) == "-") {
            eneg = true
            es = es.substring(1, es.length)
        }
        e10 = parseInt(es)
        if (eneg) { e10 = 0 - e10 }
    }
    // Split the mantissa at its point; `pt` is how many digits precede it.
    var pt = mant.length
    var di = mant.indexOf(".")
    var digits = mant
    if (di >= 0) {
        pt = di
        digits = mant.substring(0, di) + mant.substring(di + 1, mant.length)
    }
    // A leading zero run does not count as significant, and each one shifts the
    // point right by one place.
    var lead = 0
    while (lead < digits.length && digits.charAt(lead) == "0") { lead = lead + 1 }
    digits = digits.substring(lead, digits.length)
    pt = pt - lead
    // A trailing zero run is not significant either, and does not move the point.
    var end = digits.length
    while (end > 1 && digits.charAt(end - 1) == "0") { end = end - 1 }
    digits = digits.substring(0, end)
    if (digits.length == 0) { return {d: "0", e: 0} }
    return {d: digits, e: e10 + pt - 1}
}

// swFloText reports whether the WHOLE string is a decimal float, for the failable
// Double("3.5"): swift answers nil for "3.5x", where a lenient parse would stop
// at the first bad character and answer 3.5.
function swFloText(s) {
    var i = 0
    if (i < s.length && (s.charAt(i) == "+" || s.charAt(i) == "-")) { i = i + 1 }
    var d0 = i
    while (i < s.length && swIsDigit(s.charAt(i))) { i = i + 1 }
    var seen = i > d0
    if (i < s.length && s.charAt(i) == ".") {
        i = i + 1
        var d1 = i
        while (i < s.length && swIsDigit(s.charAt(i))) { i = i + 1 }
        if (i > d1) { seen = true }
    }
    if (!seen) { return false }
    if (i < s.length && (s.charAt(i) == "e" || s.charAt(i) == "E")) {
        i = i + 1
        if (i < s.length && (s.charAt(i) == "+" || s.charAt(i) == "-")) { i = i + 1 }
        var d2 = i
        while (i < s.length && swIsDigit(s.charAt(i))) { i = i + 1 }
        if (i == d2) { return false }
    }
    return i == s.length
}

function swIsDigit(c) { return c >= "0" && c <= "9" }

// ===================================================== integer conversion ===
//
// swIntFromStr is Int("17") / UInt8("300"): the failable string initializer. It
// answers undefined for anything that is not a complete, in-range decimal
// integer. The Go twin accumulates in a uint64 and rejects past 2^64; MetaJS has
// only the double, so the accumulation runs through sintOp (the floor's exact 64
// bit multiply-add, which WRAPS) and the range check below - the value read back
// as text must equal the canonical input - catches every wrap, exactly as it
// catches an out-of-width truncation. So one check does both jobs.
function swIntFromStr(s, w, u) {
    var i = 0
    var neg = false
    if (i < s.length && (s.charAt(i) == "+" || s.charAt(i) == "-")) {
        neg = s.charAt(i) == "-"
        i = i + 1
    }
    if (i >= s.length) { return undefined }
    var acc = 0
    var k = i
    var digits = 0
    while (k < s.length) {
        var c = s.charAt(k)
        if (!swIsDigit(c)) { return undefined }
        acc = sintOp(0, sintOp(2, acc, 10), c.charCodeAt(0) - 48)
        digits = digits + 1
        // 20 digits is the most a 64 bit value can have; past that the wrap the
        // Go twin rejects outright has certainly happened, and the canonical
        // check below would still catch it - this only keeps the loop honest.
        if (digits > 20) { return undefined }
        k = k + 1
    }
    var v = acc
    if (neg) { v = sintOp(1, 0, acc) }
    var out = sintConv(v, w, u)
    var back = sintStr(out)
    if (back !== swIntCanon(s, neg)) { return undefined }
    return out
}

// The canonical decimal text of a digit run - the sign and the leading zeros
// removed - for the range check above.
function swIntCanon(s, neg) {
    var i = 0
    while (i < s.length && (s.charAt(i) == "+" || s.charAt(i) == "-")) { i = i + 1 }
    while (i < s.length - 1 && s.charAt(i) == "0") { i = i + 1 }
    var d = s.substring(i, s.length)
    while (d.length > 1 && d.charAt(0) == "0") { d = d.substring(1, d.length) }
    if (d.length == 0) { return "0" }
    if (d === "0") { return "0" }
    if (neg) { return "-" + d }
    return d
}

// ===================================================== object shapes ========

function swIsObj(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (v === null) { return false }
    return typeof v == "object"
}

// A MetaJS list. A Dictionary/tuple/instance box is an object without a numeric
// `length`, so this is the same discrimination php-rt.metajs's phIsList makes.
function swIsArr(v) {
    if (!swIsObj(v)) { return false }
    if (v["__dict"] === true) { return false }
    return typeof v["length"] == "number"
}

function swIsDict(v) {
    if (!swIsObj(v)) { return false }
    if (v["__dict"] !== true) { return false }
    if (!swIsArr(v["keys"])) { return false }
    return swIsArr(v["vals"])
}

// swNumProp is the Go twin's swNumProp: the value of a numeric internal slot, or
// -1 for a shape that does not have one. (-1 rather than a second return value,
// which MetaJS has no tuple for; no slot is ever legitimately negative.)
function swNumProp(o, key) {
    var f = o[key]
    if (typeof f != "number") { return 0 - 1 }
    return f
}

function swStrArray(v) {
    if (!swIsArr(v)) { return [] }
    return v
}

// dictFind: the index of a key, or -1. The Go twin hashes; a linear scan with the
// same strictEq is observationally identical and is what layer 2 can write.
function swDictFind(keys, k) {
    var i = 0
    while (i < keys.length) {
        if (keys[i] === k) { return i }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

// swFindMember walks the __class / __super chain for a CALLABLE member, the same
// walk classFind does in swift-interpreter.abnf and swFindMember in the Go twin.
function swFindMember(v, name) {
    if (!swIsObj(v)) { return undefined }
    var c = v["__class"]
    var guard = 0
    while (guard < 64) {
        guard = guard + 1
        if (!swIsObj(c)) { return undefined }
        var mth = c[name]
        if (typeof mth == "function") { return mth }
        c = c["__super"]
    }
    return undefined
}

// ===================================================== rendering ============

// swQuote is Swift's debugDescription of a String: double quotes and the four
// escapes Swift itself emits.
function swQuote(s) {
    var out = "\""
    var i = 0
    while (i < s.length) {
        var c = s.charAt(i)
        if (c == "\\") { out = out + "\\\\" }
        else if (c == "\"") { out = out + "\\\"" }
        else if (c == "\n") { out = out + "\\n" }
        else if (c == "\t") { out = out + "\\t" }
        else if (c == "\r") { out = out + "\\r" }
        else if (c.charCodeAt(0) === 0) { out = out + "\\0" }
        else { out = out + c }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out + "\""
}

// swDesc is Swift's String(describing:): the TOP-LEVEL form, in which a String
// renders as itself. `nested` selects the quoted form, used for an element inside
// a collection, a tuple, an enum case or a struct. `depth` only guards against a
// cyclic object graph so the renderer cannot recurse forever.
function swDesc(v) { return swRender(v, false, 0) }

function swRender(v, nested, depth) {
    if (depth > 32) { return "..." }
    if (v === undefined) { return "nil" }
    if (v === null) { return "nil" }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintStr(v) }
    if (floIs(v)) { return swFloStr(floNum(v)) }
    if (typeof v == "string") {
        if (nested) { return swQuote(v) }
        return v
    }
    if (swIsArr(v)) {
        var out = "["
        var i = 0
        while (i < v.length) {
            if (i > 0) { out = out + ", " }
            out = out + swRender(v[i], true, depth + 1)
            i = i + 1
        }
        return out + "]"
    }
    if (swIsObj(v)) {
        var d = swObjDesc(v, depth)
        if (d !== undefined) { return d }
    }
    return "" + v
}

// swObjDesc renders the object shapes the two Swift grammars build. It answers
// undefined for a shape it does not know, which falls back to the generic
// ToString. The arm ORDER is the Go twin's and matters: a user description wins
// over every built-in rendering, exactly as CustomStringConvertible does.
function swObjDesc(o, depth) {
    var g = swFindMember(o, "__get_description")
    if (g !== undefined) { return swDesc(g.apply(undefined, [o])) }
    var dd = o["description"]
    if (dd !== undefined && typeof dd != "function") { return swDesc(dd) }

    // Dictionary: ["k": v, ...], and the empty one is [:].
    if (swIsDict(o)) {
        var keys = o["keys"]
        var vals = o["vals"]
        if (keys.length == 0) { return "[:]" }
        var out = "["
        var i = 0
        while (i < keys.length) {
            if (i > 0) { out = out + ", " }
            var val = undefined
            if (i < vals.length) { val = vals[i] }
            out = out + swRender(keys[i], true, depth + 1) + ": " + swRender(val, true, depth + 1)
            i = i + 1
        }
        return out + "]"
    }

    // Tuple: (1, "two") / (x: 1, y: "s"). The element labels ride along as
    // __tlabels when the tuple has any (an unlabelled slot is "").
    var tn = swNumProp(o, "__tuple")
    if (tn >= 0) {
        var labels = swStrArray(o["__tlabels"])
        var tout = "("
        var t = 0
        while (t < tn) {
            if (t > 0) { tout = tout + ", " }
            var ts = swRender(o["" + t], true, depth + 1)
            if (t < labels.length && labels[t] !== "") { ts = labels[t] + ": " + ts }
            tout = tout + ts
            t = t + 1
        }
        return tout + ")"
    }

    // An enum case: `c`, `a(1, "x")`, `b(n: 2)`. A raw-value enum still prints the
    // CASE NAME, not the raw value.
    if (o["__ecase"] === true) {
        var ename = o["__ename"]
        var evals = o["__vals"]
        if (!swIsArr(evals)) { return ename }
        if (evals.length == 0) { return ename }
        var elabels = swStrArray(o["__labels"])
        var eout = ename + "("
        var e = 0
        while (e < evals.length) {
            if (e > 0) { eout = eout + ", " }
            var es = swRender(evals[e], true, depth + 1)
            if (e < elabels.length && elabels[e] !== "") { es = elabels[e] + ": " + es }
            eout = eout + es
            e = e + 1
        }
        return eout + ")"
    }

    // A Set literal, where the grammars build one.
    if (o["__set"] === true) {
        var el = o["elems"]
        if (swIsArr(el)) {
            var sout = "["
            var q = 0
            while (q < el.length) {
                if (q > 0) { sout = sout + ", " }
                sout = sout + swRender(el[q], true, depth + 1)
                q = q + 1
            }
            return sout + "]"
        }
    }

    // The type descriptor itself (`print(P.self)`) is its name.
    if (o["__isclass"] !== undefined) {
        if (typeof o["__name"] == "string") { return o["__name"] }
    }
    if (o["__isenum"] !== undefined) {
        if (typeof o["__name"] == "string") { return o["__name"] }
    }

    // An instance. A STRUCT prints memberwise - P(x: 1, y: 2) - because Swift
    // synthesizes that reflection-based description for every struct; a CLASS
    // prints its bare type name (real Swift qualifies it with the module, which
    // this runtime does not have - see the Go twin's header).
    var cls = o["__class"]
    if (!swIsObj(cls)) { return undefined }
    var name = cls["__name"]
    if (typeof name != "string") { return undefined }
    if (name === "") { return undefined }
    if (cls["__struct"] !== true) { return name }
    var flds = swStrArray(cls["__fields"])
    var fout = name + "("
    var k = 0
    while (k < flds.length) {
        if (k > 0) { fout = fout + ", " }
        fout = fout + flds[k] + ": " + swRender(o[flds[k]], true, depth + 1)
        k = k + 1
    }
    return fout + ")"
}

// ===================================================== equality =============
//
// swEqual is Swift's ==. Array, Dictionary and tuple are VALUE types in Swift, so
// they compare element by element; js_seq compares them by IDENTITY and answered
// false for every one. An enum case compares by name plus associated values.
function swEqual(l, r, depth) {
    if (depth > 32) { return false }
    if (l === undefined || l === null || r === undefined || r === null) {
        var ln = l === undefined || l === null
        var rn = r === undefined || r === null
        return ln && rn
    }
    // A float box compares BY VALUE and ACROSS the box boundary: `1.0 == 1` holds
    // in Swift (the untyped literal takes the Double type). It has to be spelled
    // before the integer path, which would compare 1.5 against Int8(1) as the
    // truncated 1.
    if (floIs(l) || floIs(r)) {
        if (!swIsNumeric(l)) { return false }
        if (!swIsNumeric(r)) { return false }
        return swNum(l) === swNum(r)
    }
    // A sized-integer box is a value of its own, so two integers compare BY VALUE
    // here rather than falling through to strictEq, which would call a box and a
    // plain number different. This is giEq.
    if (sintIs(l) || sintIs(r)) {
        if (swIsNumeric(l) && swIsNumeric(r)) { return sintCmp(l, r) === 0 }
        return l === r
    }
    if (swIsArr(l)) {
        if (!swIsArr(r)) { return false }
        if (l.length !== r.length) { return false }
        var i = 0
        while (i < l.length) {
            if (!swEqual(l[i], r[i], depth + 1)) { return false }
            i = i + 1
        }
        return true
    }
    if (swIsObj(l) && swIsObj(r)) {
        if (l === r) { return true }
        if (swIsDict(l)) {
            if (!swIsDict(r)) { return false }
            var lk = l["keys"]
            var lv = l["vals"]
            var rk = r["keys"]
            var rv = r["vals"]
            if (lk.length !== rk.length) { return false }
            // A Dictionary is unordered, so every entry of the left must have a
            // matching entry somewhere in the right.
            var a = 0
            while (a < lk.length) {
                var found = false
                var b = 0
                while (b < rk.length) {
                    if (swEqual(lk[a], rk[b], depth + 1) && swEqual(lv[a], rv[b], depth + 1)) { found = true }
                    b = b + 1
                }
                if (!found) { return false }
                a = a + 1
            }
            return true
        }
        var ln2 = swNumProp(l, "__tuple")
        if (ln2 >= 0) {
            var rn2 = swNumProp(r, "__tuple")
            if (rn2 !== ln2) { return false }
            var t = 0
            while (t < ln2) {
                if (!swEqual(l["" + t], r["" + t], depth + 1)) { return false }
                t = t + 1
            }
            return true
        }
        if (l["__ecase"] === true) {
            if (r["__ecase"] !== true) { return false }
            if (l["__ename"] !== r["__ename"]) { return false }
            var lvals = l["__vals"]
            var rvals = r["__vals"]
            if (!swIsArr(lvals) || !swIsArr(rvals)) { return !swIsArr(lvals) && !swIsArr(rvals) }
            if (lvals.length !== rvals.length) { return false }
            var e = 0
            while (e < lvals.length) {
                if (!swEqual(lvals[e], rvals[e], depth + 1)) { return false }
                e = e + 1
            }
            return true
        }
        return false
    }
    return l === r
}

// ===================================================== the exports ==========
//
// Everything below is a TOP-LEVEL `function js_*`, which is what -rt-lib exports
// under its own C symbol. The order follows abnf/jsrtswift.go, with the
// language-NEUTRAL externs of abnf/jsrt.go first.

// ----- the language-NEUTRAL ten ---------------------------------------------
// None of these is Swift's. They live in abnf/jsrt.go, several languages emit
// them, and they are here only because the C floor does not have them yet - see
// the extern split in docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3.

// Kotlin's Char from its code. THE FLOOR HAS NO jsChar (which is why js_jadd and
// js_jfstep were left out of it), and Swift does not need one: see point 3 of the
// header. The only two call sites are the emitter's sw_nfd, which hands the
// result straight to js_jadd, where the Go twin's jsChar goes through
// rt.toString - and that is the character. So a one-character string is the same
// value at every point Swift can observe.
function js_char(cp) {
    if (cp < 65536) { return String.fromCharCode(cp) }
    var v = cp - 65536
    return String.fromCharCode(55296 + Math.floor(v / 1024)) + String.fromCharCode(56320 + (v % 1024))
}

// The code of a Char, and the identity for everything else. The Go twin is
// rt.toNumber, which reads a jsChar's code point; here the argument is the
// one-character string js_kindex answered, so the code point is read off it.
function js_char_code(v) {
    if (typeof v != "string") { return sintNum(v) }
    if (v.length == 0) { return 0 / 0 }
    return v.charCodeAt(0)
}

// Kotlin's indexed read s[i]: a String yields the character at that CODE UNIT
// index, everything else reads the member like js_get. The Go twin answers a
// jsChar built from []rune(strAt(s,i))[0], so a LONE SURROGATE - half of an
// astral pair - becomes U+FFFD, which is exactly how the emitter's sw_gcount
// recognises a pair. That mapping is reproduced here rather than left to the
// engine, which would hand back the surrogate itself.
function js_kindex(s, i) {
    if (typeof s != "string") { return s[i] }
    if (i < 0 || i >= s.length) { fail("string index out of range") }
    var c = s.charCodeAt(i)
    if (c >= 55296 && c <= 57343) { return String.fromCharCode(65533) }
    return String.fromCharCode(c)
}

// An empty Python dict / Go map / Swift Dictionary handle.
// js_dict_new is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs (six identical copies, no knobs).

// Sequence indexing (negative wraps) and dict lookup. Swift's emitter routes
// every subscript through this (core.indexExt).
function js_pyget(c, k) {
    if (swIsDict(c)) {
        var i = swDictFind(c["keys"], k)
        if (i < 0) { fail("KeyError: " + swDesc(k)) }
        return c["vals"][i]
    }
    var idx = swNum(k)
    if (swIsArr(c)) {
        if (idx < 0) { idx = idx + c.length }
        if (idx < 0 || idx >= c.length) { fail("list index out of range") }
        return c[idx]
    }
    if (typeof c == "string") {
        if (idx < 0) { idx = idx + c.length }
        if (idx < 0 || idx >= c.length) { fail("string index out of range") }
        return c.charAt(idx)
    }
    fail("indexing a " + typeof c)
    return undefined
}

// List element (negative wraps) or dict entry assignment.
function js_pyset(c, k, v) {
    if (swIsDict(c)) {
        var keys = c["keys"]
        var i = swDictFind(keys, k)
        if (i >= 0) { c["vals"][i] = v } else {
            keys.push(k)
            c["vals"].push(v)
        }
        return undefined
    }
    if (!swIsArr(c)) { fail("item assignment on a " + typeof c) }
    var idx = swNum(k)
    if (idx < 0) { idx = idx + c.length }
    if (idx < 0 || idx >= c.length) { fail("list assignment index out of range") }
    c[idx] = v
    return undefined
}

// len() for strings, lists, sets and dicts. Python counts CODE POINTS in a
// string, not UTF-16 code units - but Swift's own .count goes through the
// emitter's sw_gcount (grapheme clusters), so the only strings that reach this
// arm are the ones the emitter measures in code UNITS on purpose.
function js_pylen(v) {
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v.length }
    if (swIsDict(v)) { return v["keys"].length }
    if (swIsObj(v) && v["__set"] !== undefined) {
        var el = v["elems"]
        if (swIsArr(el)) { return el.length }
    }
    if (swIsArr(v)) { return v.length }
    fail("len() of a " + typeof v)
    return 0
}

// e is T. The Go twin is rt.isTypeName, and the SET OF NAMES it knows is what is
// reproduced here - including the two facts a reader would not guess: an
// Int8...UInt64 box is NOT an "Int" (the arm tests a plain float64) and a boxed
// Double is NOT a "Double". Swift's emitter handles its own enum and class names
// through the descriptor walk at the bottom, which is where `x is MyType` lands.
// js_is_type is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs, via the rtkIsChar/rtkIsArr hooks.

// Java/C# +: string concat, 32 bit add for two int values, float add otherwise.
// swift-to-llvm-ir.abnf emits it for &+ (Swift's wrapping add) AND for its own
// loop counters and string builders. The float arm needs the boxed double, which
// the floor HAS - so unlike php-rt.metajs's, this one is complete.
// js_jadd is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs, via the rtkStr/rtkNum hooks above.

function swJavaStr(v) {
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v }
    if (v === null) { return "null" }
    if (v === undefined) { return "null" }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return v ? "true" : "false" }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintStr(v) }
    if (floIs(v)) { return floStr(v) }
    return "" + v
}



// (target, method name, args array). The Go twin (rt.memberCall in abnf/jsrt.go)
// is a LANGUAGE-NEUTRAL dispatcher over strings, dicts, lists, generators and
// instances. Every arm swift-to-llvm-ir.abnf can reach is here; the GENERATOR arm
// is the one that is not, and it cannot be - a generator needs a coroutine
// primitive the floor does not have (WALL 1 of docs/runtime-next-plan.md). Swift
// emits no js_genfn/js_yield at all (`grep -c` is 0 in the grammar), so nothing
// in this language can reach it.
function js_mcall(recv, name, args) {
    if (typeof recv == "string") {
        if (name === "length") { return recv.length }
        if (name === "charAt") {
            var ci = swNum(args[0])
            if (ci < 0 || ci >= recv.length) { fail("charAt out of range") }
            return recv.charAt(ci)
        }
        if (name === "equals") { return recv === args[0] }
        if (name === "substring") {
            var bg = 0
            var en = recv.length
            if (args.length > 0) { bg = swNum(args[0]) }
            if (args.length > 1) { en = swNum(args[1]) }
            if (bg < 0) { bg = 0 }
            if (en > recv.length) { en = recv.length }
            if (bg > en) { return "" }
            return recv.substring(bg, en)
        }
        if (name === "indexOf") { return recv.indexOf("" + args[0]) }
        if (name === "isEmpty") { return recv.length == 0 }
        fail("unknown String method: " + name)
    }
    if (swIsArr(recv)) { return swListMethod(recv, name, args) }
    if (swIsDict(recv)) {
        if (name === "keys") { return swCopyArr(recv["keys"]) }
        if (name === "values") { return swCopyArr(recv["vals"]) }
        if (name === "get") {
            var gi = swDictFind(recv["keys"], args[0])
            if (gi >= 0) { return recv["vals"][gi] }
            if (args.length > 1) { return args[1] }
            return undefined
        }
        fail("unknown dict method '" + name + "'")
    }
    if (swIsObj(recv)) {
        var cls = recv["__class"]
        if (cls !== undefined) {
            var c = cls
            var guard = 0
            while (guard < 64) {
                guard = guard + 1
                if (!swIsObj(c)) { guard = 99 } else {
                    var mm = c[name]
                    if (typeof mm == "function") {
                        var av = [recv]
                        var i = 0
                        while (i < args.length) {
                            av.push(args[i])
                            i = i + 1
                        }
                        return mm.apply(undefined, av)
                    }
                    c = c["__super"]
                }
            }
            fail("unknown method '" + name + "' on an instance")
        }
        var f = recv[name]
        if (typeof f == "function") { return f.apply(undefined, args) }
        fail("unknown method '" + name + "'")
    }
    fail("method call '" + name + "' on a " + typeof recv)
    return undefined
}

function swCopyArr(a) {
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        out.push(a[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// The Kotlin/Python list methods of rt.memberCall, in its order.
function swListMethod(o, name, args) {
    if (name === "add") {
        o.push(args[0])
        return true
    }
    if (name === "append") {
        o.push(args[0])
        return undefined
    }
    if (name === "pop" || name === "removeLast") {
        if (o.length == 0) { fail("pop from empty list") }
        return o.pop()
    }
    if (name === "size") { return o.length }
    if (name === "get") {
        var gi = swNum(args[0])
        if (gi < 0 || gi >= o.length) { fail("list index out of range") }
        return o[gi]
    }
    if (name === "contains") {
        var ci = 0
        while (ci < o.length) {
            if (o[ci] === args[0]) { return true }
            ci = ci + 1
        }
        return false
    }
    if (name === "map") {
        var mo = []
        var mi = 0
        while (mi < o.length) {
            mo.push(args[0].apply(undefined, [o[mi]]))
            mi = mi + 1
        }
        return mo
    }
    if (name === "filter") {
        var fo = []
        var fi = 0
        while (fi < o.length) {
            if (args[0].apply(undefined, [o[fi]])) { fo.push(o[fi]) }
            fi = fi + 1
        }
        return fo
    }
    if (name === "sumOf") {
        var sum = 0
        var si = 0
        while (si < o.length) {
            sum = rtWrap32(sum + Math.trunc(swNum(args[0].apply(undefined, [o[si]]))))
            si = si + 1
        }
        return sum
    }
    if (name === "forEach") {
        var ei = 0
        while (ei < o.length) {
            args[0].apply(undefined, [o[ei]])
            ei = ei + 1
        }
        return undefined
    }
    if (name === "count") {
        if (args.length == 0) { return o.length }
        var n = 0
        var ni = 0
        while (ni < o.length) {
            if (args[0].apply(undefined, [o[ni]])) { n = n + 1 }
            ni = ni + 1
        }
        return n
    }
    if (name === "any") {
        var ai = 0
        while (ai < o.length) {
            if (args[0].apply(undefined, [o[ai]])) { return true }
            ai = ai + 1
        }
        return false
    }
    fail("unknown list method '" + name + "'")
    return undefined
}

// ----- Swift's own twenty-one ------------------------------------------------

// String(describing:) / "\(v)" / the thing print writes.
function js_swdesc(v) { return swDesc(v) }

// String(x) / String(describing: x): the same text.
function js_swstr(v) { return swDesc(v) }

// Swift's print(_:separator:terminator:). args is the argument ARRAY, sep the
// separator and term the terminator; nil means the default (" " and "\n").
function js_swprint(args, sep, term) {
    var s = " "
    if (sep !== undefined && sep !== null) { s = swDesc(sep) }
    var t = "\n"
    if (term !== undefined && term !== null) { t = swDesc(term) }
    var out = ""
    var i = 0
    while (i < args.length) {
        if (i > 0) { out = out + s }
        out = out + swDesc(args[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    print(out + t)
    return undefined
}

// Swift's ==. js_seq compares an Array, a Dictionary and a tuple by IDENTITY; in
// Swift all three are value types and compare element by element.
function js_sweq(l, r) { return swEqual(l, r, 0) }

// ----- parameter packs (SE-0393) -----
// A `repeat E` expansion produces a TUPLE of the per-element results, and
// `for x in repeat each p` is the one place a tuple is walked as a sequence.
function js_swtuple(arr) {
    var o = {__tuple: arr.length}
    var i = 0
    while (i < arr.length) {
        o["" + i] = arr[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    return o
}

function js_swtuparr(o) {
    if (!swIsObj(o)) { return o }
    var n = swNumProp(o, "__tuple")
    if (n < 0) { return o }
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        out.push(o["" + i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// ----- Swift's arithmetic -----
// One binary arithmetic or bitwise operator: (op, left, right). Two INTEGRAL
// operands go to the sized-integer tag, which evaluates at the result type's
// width and wraps to it; an operand that is a FLOAT BOX pulls the whole operation
// into floating point and the result is a box again.
function swOpCode(op) {
    if (op === "+") { return 0 }
    if (op === "-") { return 1 }
    if (op === "*") { return 2 }
    if (op === "/") { return 3 }
    if (op === "%") { return 4 }
    if (op === "&") { return 5 }
    if (op === "|") { return 6 }
    if (op === "^") { return 7 }
    if (op === "&^") { return 8 }
    if (op === "<<") { return 9 }
    if (op === ">>") { return 10 }
    return 0 - 1
}

// giArith, through the floor's tag: an operator the table does not have answers
// sintOp's own zero, which is what the Go twin's `rt.fail` guard would not reach
// from an emitter that only ever passes the eleven.
function swGiArith(op, l, r) {
    var c = swOpCode(op)
    if (c < 0) { fail("js_swarith: unknown operator " + op) }
    if ((c == 3 || c == 4) && swNum(r) === 0) { fail("integer divide by zero") }
    return sintOp(c, l, r)
}

function js_swarith(op, l, r) {
    if (swWhole(l) && swWhole(r)) { return swGiArith(op, l, r) }
    if (op === "+") {
        if (typeof l == "string" || typeof r == "string") { return swDesc(l) + swDesc(r) }
        // Array + Array is CONCATENATION in Swift, where the shared jsAdd coerced
        // both to text and answered "1,23".
        if (swIsArr(l) && swIsArr(r)) {
            var out = swCopyArr(l)
            var i = 0
            while (i < r.length) {
                out.push(r[i])
                i = i + 1
            }
            return out
        }
    }
    var x = swNum(l)
    var y = swNum(r)
    if (op === "+") { return swMkFlo(x + y) }
    if (op === "-") { return swMkFlo(x - y) }
    if (op === "*") { return swMkFlo(x * y) }
    if (op === "/") { return swMkFlo(x / y) }
    if (op === "%") { return swMkFlo(x % y) }
    return swGiArith(op, l, r)
